---
// Fact-forward pass (matches the corrected helical-piers-deep-dive.astro
// treatment). Covers both compaction grouting and mud jacking. Corrected
// per Tim's review: these are NOT the same mix aimed at different targets --
// slump, mobility, and placement all differ. Compaction grouting stays low
// slump (roughly 0-2in) to displace and densify soil in place; mud jacking
// is mixed more flowable so it can travel to fill a void under a slab and
// build enough hydrostatic pressure to lift it. Re-verified against MFRC's
// compaction-grouting page and rewritten in TLS's own structure. Added the physical-connection
// vs. ground-improvement framing Tim asked for: piering makes a physical
// connection to bearing material, grouting improves the ground itself --
// physical connection is usually the stronger repair when it's achievable,
// ground improvement is the right call, not a fallback, when it isn't.
// No misconceptions/Q&A section (dropped sitewide). [TIM: ...] marks
// spots needing real NM numbers/field experience.
//
// Per Tim: added the depth/overburden "Goldilocks" dynamic -- too deep
// and cost climbs fast (more injection points/stages/volume), too
// shallow and there isn't enough overburden to confine the grout as it
// displaces, so it migrates toward the surface instead of compacting
// the target zone. Added the global-treatment advantage (soil
// improvement can treat a broader zone at once vs. a cluster of
// individual pier points). Added a real-consequences callout: grouting
// soil that can't support the structure doesn't just fail to help, it
// can make movement continue or accelerate, since the underlying
// condition was never addressed. The installation-section placeholder
// on frequency/typical job/equipment is still open -- this answered a
// different, more consequential question instead.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Compaction Grouting & Mud Jacking in New Mexico | TLS Foundations';
const summary =
  'Compaction grouting and mud jacking come from the same grout family but are mixed differently for two different jobs -- compaction grouting stays low-mobility (roughly 0 to 2 inches of slump) to displace and densify soil in place, while mud jacking is mixed more flowable so it can travel to fill a void under a slab and build enough hydrostatic pressure to lift it. Neither creates a pile; neither is foam. Compaction grouting is ground improvement, not a shortcut around understanding the ground -- it depends entirely on soil that can actually take displacement without fracturing or migrating somewhere it shouldn\'t.';
const description =
  'What compaction grouting and mud jacking are, how the mix and mobility differ even though they share the same grout family, and when New Mexico soil conditions call for one over the other.';

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Compaction Grouting & Mud Jacking in New Mexico',
    description,
    about: description,
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TLS Foundations' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TLS Foundations' },
    url: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/compaction-grouting-deep-dive',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Foundation Repair Methods', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/foundation-repair-methods' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Compaction Grouting & Mud Jacking', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/compaction-grouting-deep-dive' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  footer="minimal"
  schema={schema}
  summary={summary}
  pageName="Compaction Grouting & Mud Jacking"
  nextPage={{ path: '/foundation-repair-methods', name: 'Foundation Repair Methods' }}
>
  <div class="tls-article" id="top">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero-inner">
        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Methods · Compaction Grouting &amp; Mud Jacking</span>
        <h1>Compaction Grouting &amp; Mud Jacking —<br /><em>Same Family, Different Mix</em></h1>
        <p class="page-hero-sub">Ground improvement by displacement, not a pile and not foam. Same grout family, mixed differently — stiffer to displace soil in place, more flowable to fill a void and lift a slab.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-outer">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="article-lede">"Grouting" gets used loosely enough on estimates that it stops meaning anything specific. This page is about the one mechanism that matters: a stiff, low-mobility grout that displaces the material around it instead of soaking into it.</p>

        <div class="article-section" id="what-they-are">
          <h2>Stiffer to Displace, <em>Looser to Lift</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Compaction grouting and mud jacking come from the same family of grout — a cement-based mix, not concrete, not foam — but they're not the same mix, because they're not doing the same job.</p>
          <p>Compaction grouting stays low-mobility, roughly 0 to 2 inches of slump, meant to stay near the injection point and form a bulb, or a string of bulbs, rather than soak into ordinary soil pores the way a thinner grout would. As each bulb grows under controlled pressure, it displaces the ground around it, and in suitable material that displacement increases density and stiffness. Mud jacking needs the opposite behavior: mixed more flowable so it can actually travel to fill a void beneath a slab, then build enough hydrostatic pressure to lift the concrete back into place. Slump, mobility, and placement all differ between the two — related family, different mix, different job.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="how-they-work">
          <h2>Ground Improvement, <em>Not a Substitute for a Pier</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Neither of these creates a conventional pile that carries building load down to a distant deep layer — they're meant to improve or support what's already there, not replace it with something new.</p>
          <p>That's the real distinction between this method and piering, not a matter of which is "better." A pier makes a physical connection from the footing to competent bearing material — it's usually the stronger repair when that connection is achievable. Grouting doesn't connect to anything; it improves the ground itself. That makes it the right call, not a fallback, when a physical connection isn't achievable or isn't what the soil condition actually calls for.</p>
          <p>For compaction grouting specifically, what you get depends on the soil itself, the treatment depths and pattern, the injection sequence, pressure and volume control, and monitoring throughout. It's ground improvement, not "adding concrete underground" as a slogan — the mechanism is displacement and densification of material that's already lost density, and uncontrolled volume can lift or fracture more than the target zone, which is why planned, monitored injection is the whole point rather than an open-ended pour.</p>
          <p>There's a real sweet spot to depth, too — almost a Goldilocks problem. Go too deep and cost climbs fast, since reaching a deeper target means more injection points, more stages, more volume placed. Go too shallow and there isn't enough overburden — the weight of soil sitting above the injection zone — to actually confine the grout as it displaces; without that confinement, the grout can migrate toward the surface instead of compacting the target zone the way it's supposed to. The right depth, in the right soil, is what makes this the most effective option on a given job — not a given on every one. Because it's a soil-improvement procedure rather than a series of individual point connections, it can also treat a broader zone at once, a genuinely global approach where a cluster of separate piers would only address isolated points.</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Getting this wrong has real consequences</span>
            <p>Compaction grouting only works on soil that's actually capable of being displaced and densified. Pumping grout into ground that genuinely can't support the structure's weight doesn't fix that — it isn't a neutral non-event. The structure can keep moving afterward, sometimes at a faster rate than before, because the underlying soil condition was never actually addressed. Understanding the soil profile before committing to this method isn't optional — it's the difference between a repair and a more expensive version of the same problem.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Not the same tool as foam</span>
            <p>Compaction grouting is a different tool from foam injection, even though both can be used to address a settled slab. Foam is an expanding polyurethane resin that fills a void; compaction grout is a stiff cement-based material that displaces and compacts the ground around it. They're not interchangeable, and a bid that just says "grouting" or "soil stabilization" hasn't actually named a method — ask what mechanism is planned.</p>
          </div>
          <details class="deeper">
            <summary class="deeper-toggle">
              <span>Going Deeper — For the Technically Curious</span>
              <span class="deeper-toggle-icon">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="deeper-body">
              <p>"Low slump" isn't a loose description here — it's the defining property. The ASCE/Geo-Institute guideline puts the working range at under 2 inches of slump, with a practical field limit around 1.5 inches, using a modified test method since the standard slump-cone procedure doesn't suit a mix this stiff. The industry term for this material is <strong>Low Mobility Grouting</strong> (also called Limited Mobility Displacement Grouting) — stiff enough to stay put and displace the ground around it rather than flow into it.</p>
              <p>Slump alone doesn't guarantee that behavior, though — the guideline is explicit that aggregate gradation matters just as much: enough silt-sized material for mobility control, enough sand and gravel for internal friction. A mix that benches stiff in a slump test can still behave like a fluid once it's actually under pump pressure if the gradation is wrong.</p>
              <p>One honest gap worth naming: unlike the piering methods, the IBC has no numbered subsection written for compaction grouting at all. Inspection instead runs through the project's geotechnical report and the general soils provisions in Section 1705.6 — real oversight, just not a citation with its own line item in the code.</p>
              <p class="deeper-cite">Source: ASCE/Geo-Institute Compaction Grouting Guideline (2007); IBC Section 1705.6, Soils.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="scope">
          <h2>What Rules Either One <em>Out</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">An active, unaddressed leak, roof runoff, grading, or irrigation problem still driving the movement.</p>
          <p>A foundation that actually needs a defined deep load path rather than improved shallow or intermediate support. Nearby foundations, utilities, and slabs that haven't been accounted for — displacement can move more than just the target zone.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="installation">
          <h2>Grid, Depths, and <em>Controlled Volume</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Both are injected through a grid of small-diameter holes at planned locations, depths, and stages, with volume and pressure controlled and monitored throughout — not a single injection point, and not an open-ended pour.</p>
          <p>[TIM: how often does this actually come up in your NM work, and what does a typical job look like when it does — equipment, timeline, anything homeowners should expect that's different from the piering jobs?]</p>
        </div>

        <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>

        <p class="closing-link"><a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="body-link">← Back to Foundation Repair Methods</a> · <a href="/glossary#compaction-grouting" class="body-link">Glossary: Compaction Grouting →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

